WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.957
A very common beginner mistake is writing equals NULL when trying to find missing values.

00:00:05.957 --> 00:00:10.927
The problem is conceptual. Equality compares two known values.

00:00:10.927 --> 00:00:13.462
NULL means the value is unknown.

00:00:13.462 --> 00:00:18.677
Asking whether something equals an unknown value does not produce a definite answer.

00:00:18.677 --> 00:00:23.177
Even when the column itself is NULL, SQL does not say TRUE.

00:00:23.177 --> 00:00:28.012
It says UNKNOWN. The same problem occurs with not equal NULL.

00:00:28.012 --> 00:00:31.067
SQL still cannot prove the relationship.

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:35.087
That is why SQL gives us special predicates: IS NULL and IS NOT NULL.

00:00:35.087 --> 00:00:40.527
Think about a form where an emergency contact has never been entered.

00:00:40.527 --> 00:00:46.217
We are not asking whether the contact value is literally equal to some value called NULL.

00:00:46.217 --> 00:00:49.812
We are asking whether there is no known value stored.

00:00:49.812 --> 00:00:53.197
IS NULL expresses that question directly.

00:00:53.197 --> 00:00:56.947
Run the example and look at the calculated comparisons.

00:00:56.947 --> 00:01:03.322
You will see NULL results rather than useful true-or-false answers when ordinary equality is used.

00:01:03.322 --> 00:01:07.767
This is one of those SQL habits worth learning early and keeping forever:

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:13.097
if your question is about missingness, use IS NULL or IS NOT NULL.

00:01:13.097 --> 00:01:17.697
Do not use equals NULL, and do not use not-equal NULL.

00:01:17.697 --> 00:01:23.256
Even if the syntax is accepted, the logic will not give the result you probably intend.
